          "id": "blk_yv1zp3c1t1",
          "type": "heading",
          "v": 1,
          "fields": {
            "text": "How a church basement became a pantry",
            "level": "h2",
            "size": "auto",
            "eyebrow": "Our story"
          },
          "variant": "plain",
          "style": {
            "align": {
              "d": "center"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "blk_zdvsjd6dqe",
          "type": "text",
          "v": 1,
          "fields": {
            "content": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "In the winter of 2004, the steel-mill pensions on our block were cut in half. Margaret Oyelaran, a retired school nurse, noticed that her neighbors were skipping meals so they could heat their homes. She set up a folding table in the basement of St. Augustine's, borrowed a van, and started giving away groceries on Saturday mornings."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Word travelled. Within a year there were forty volunteers and a waiting list for help with leaking roofs and broken furnaces. Homework Harbor followed in 2012, when parents who came to the pantry asked for somewhere safe for their kids after school. We still ask the same question before starting anything new: what do our neighbors say they need?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          "variant": "drop-cap"
        }
      ]
    },
